<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Court</h1><p class="st-meta">1336 CE – 1392 CE · capital Kyoto</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The line of Japanese emperors in Kyoto backed by the Ashikaga shoguns during the 14th-century schism, from whom the present imperial family descends.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a>, Takauji placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-komyo">Komyo</a>, of the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimyoin-line">Jimyoin</a> branch of the imperial house, on the throne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1336. Go-Daigo fled with the sacred regalia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshino-nara">Yoshino</a>, splitting the court in two.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Northern Court depended on Ashikaga military power and had little independent authority, but it held the capital and conducted court ceremonies and appointments. Its emperors included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-komyo">Komyo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-suko">Suko</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-kogon">Go-Kogon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-enyu">Go-En'yu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-komatsu">Go-Komatsu</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanno-disturbance">Kanno disturbance</a> of 1350 to 1352 Southern forces seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and carried off three Northern ex-emperors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1392 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> negotiated reunification: the Southern emperor handed the regalia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-komatsu">Go-Komatsu</a>, ending the schism. In 1911 the Meiji government declared the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-court">Southern Court</a> legitimate, though later emperors descend from the Northern line.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1336 CE – 1392 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.29%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1336 CE – 1392 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kyoto</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Komyo, Go-En'yu, Go-Komatsu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shinto, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hokucho, Northern Dynasty (Japan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=northern-court" aria-label="Northern Court on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8121 911 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Court lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8441 1167L8441 1168M8441 1167L8441 1168M8441 1167L8455 1188"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0M8441 1168h0M8455 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kyoto. <a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=northern-court">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
